      "text": "To find the minimum value of the quadratic function \\( z = x^2 + 2y^2 + 6x - 4y + 22 \\), we will use the concept of completing the square for both \\( x \\) and \\( y \\).\n\nFirst, consider the \\( x \\)-terms: \\( x^2 + 6x \\). To complete the square, we add and subtract \\((6/2)^2 = 9\\):\n\\[\nx^2 + 6x = (x + 3)^2 - 9.\n\\]\n\nNext, consider the \\( y \\)-terms: \\( 2y^2 - 4y \\). To complete the square, we factor out the coefficient of \\( y^2 \\) outside and complete the square within:\n\\[\n2y^2 - 4y = 2(y^2 - 2y).\n\\]\nComplete the square inside the parentheses:\n\\[\ny^2 - 2y = (y - 1)^2 - 1.\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\n2(y^2 - 2y) = 2((y - 1)^2 - 1) = 2(y - 1)^2 - 2.\n\\]\n\nNow integrate the adjusted terms back into the original expression:\n\\[\nz = (x + 3)^2 - 9 + 2((y - 1)^2 - 1) + 22.\n\\]\n\nCombine the constants:\n\\[\nz = (x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2 - 9 + 22.\n\\]\n\nImprove this expression by simplifying constants:\n\\[\nz = (x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2 + 13.\n\\]\n\nNotice that \\((x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2\\) is a sum of squares and thus non-negative because the squares of real numbers are always non-negative:\n\\[\n(x + 3)^2 \\geq 0, \\quad 2(y - 1)^2 \\geq 0.\n\\]\n\nTherefore,\n\\[\n(x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2 \\geq 0.\n\\]\n\nThus, the minimum value of:\n\\[\n(x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2 + 13\n\\]\n is when \\((x + 3)^2 = 0\\) and \\(2(y - 1)^2 = 0\\), i.e.,\n\\[\n(x + 3)^2 = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 2(y - 1)^2 = 0,\n\\]\nleading us to,\n\\[\nx + 3 = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y - 1 = 0.\n\\]\n\nSo,\n\\[\nx = -3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y = 1.\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\( x = -3 \\) and \\( y = 1 \\) back into the original function to find the minimum value of \\( z \\):\n\\[\nz = (-3)^2 + 2(1)^2 + 6(-3) - 4(1) + 22.\n\\]\n\nCalculate each term individually:\n\\[\nz = 9 + 2 - 18 - 4 + 22.\n\\]\n\nCombine all terms:\n\\[\nz = 9 + 2 - 18 - 4 + 22 = 11.\n\\]\n\nThe minimum value of \\( z \\) is therefore:\n\\[\n\\boxed{11}.\n\\]",
