      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "First, we want to rewrite the expression of $z$: $z=x^2+2y^2+6x-4y+22$.\n\nThis problem can be generalized as minimizing a quadratic function in two variables. To do this, we want to apply the \"quadratic inequality method.\"\n\nWLOG, we assume $x^2+2y^2<22$. Then, the quantity expression $y^2-6y+22-2y^2-6x+2x$ is minimized.\n\nWe construct the system of equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\ny &= -\\frac{6}{2}\\\\\nx &= \\frac{6}{2}\n\\end{cases}.\n\\]\n\nSolving this gives the point $(x,y) = (-3, 1)$, which lies on the curve $x^2 + 2y^2 = 22$.\n\nNow, we substitute these values back into the expression for $z$:\n\\[\nz = x^2 + 2y^2 - 6x - 4y + 22 = 9 + 2 - 2 = 9.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $z$ is $\\boxed{9}$.",
